Type B: Math Operations (disassembler-dump0_c)
plain
mov eax, 0x9fe1a      → eax = 654,874
imul eax, 0x4         → eax = 654,874 × 4 = 2,619,496
add eax, 0x1f5        → eax = 2,619,496 + 501 = 2,619,997
→ eax = 2,619,997
Type C: Conditional (disassembler-dump0_d)
plain
cmp [rbp-0x4], 0x2710     → compare 654,874 with 10,000
jle <main+37>             → jump if ≤ (FALSE, 654874 > 10000)
sub [rbp-0x4], 0x65       → execute this: 654,874 - 101 = 654,773
jmp <main+41>             → skip the add
mov eax, [rbp-0x4]        → eax = 654,773
→ eax = 654,773
Type D: Loop (debugger0_b)
plain
result = 0x1e0da (123,098)
limit = 0x25f (607)
counter = 0

while (counter < limit):
    result = result + counter
    counter = counter + 1

return result
Loop adds: 0 + 1 + 2 + ... + 606 = 183,921
Final: 123,098 + 183,921 = 307,019
→ eax = 307,019
5. GDB Commands
Table
Command	What It Does
gdb filename	Open file in GDB
set disassembly-flavor intel	Use Intel syntax
disassemble main	Show main function assembly
break *main+15	Set breakpoint at address
run	Execute program
stepi or si	Execute 1 instruction
continue or c	Continue to next breakpoint
info registers eax	Show eax value
print/d $eax	Print eax in decimal
print/x $eax	Print eax in hex
quit	Exit GDB
6. Quick GDB Session Template
bash
# Terminal
gdb debugger0_a
gdb
# Inside GDB
set disassembly-flavor intel
disassemble main

# If simple: just read the mov eax value
# If complex: set breakpoint and run
break *main+56
run
info registers eax
quit
7. Flag Format
plain
picoCTF{decimal_value}
Always decimal, never hex!
8. Loop Detection Checklist
Look for this pattern:
plain
1. Initialize variables
2. jmp forward (to condition check)
3. ← LOOP BODY (work + increment)
4. Condition check: cmp + conditional jump back
5. After loop: mov eax, result
